## 1. Sprint Overview

The project is delivered across 5 sprints of 1 week each (5 weeks total). Each sprint has a clear goal, a set of user stories drawn from the requirement-analysis document, and a designated owner, with a supporting member where the module depends on shared groundwork (e.g. database schema, authentication).

| Sprint | Duration | Sprint Goal |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Sprint 1 | Week 1 | Project setup, authentication, and role-based access for all user types |
| Sprint 2 | Week 2 | Organizers can create and manage events; attendees can browse/search them |
| Sprint 3 | Week 3 | Attendees can register within capacity limits; full waitlist join flow available |
| Sprint 4 | Week 4 | Paid registrations go through mock payment; cancellations trigger refunds and auto-promote the waitlist |
| Sprint 5 | Week 5 | QR check-in, role-based dashboards, and final validation/polish |

---

## 2. Sprint 1 — Foundation: Auth, Roles & Database Schema

**Owner:** Dhrubo Dutta (Auth & Roles). **Supporting:** Parthib Datta Muhuri (initial database schema).

| User Story | Tasks | Story Points |
| --- | --- | --- |
| As a new user, I want to register and log in so that I can access the system. | Set up ASP.NET Core Identity; build register/login pages | 5 |
| As the system, I want three distinct roles so that permissions are properly separated. | Configure Attendee/Organizer/Admin roles; role-based route guards | 5 |
| As a developer, I want the core database schema in place so that other modules can build on it. | Create Users, Events (skeleton) tables via EF Core migrations | 3 |

